---
import ProjectPage from "../../layouts/ProjectPage.astro";

const docs = "https://super-yojan.dev/ARGOS/";
const repo = "https://github.com/Super-Yojan/ARGOS";
---

<ProjectPage
    title="ARGOS"
    description="ARGOS is a native fleet operator for Terra. Rust and Swift on macOS 14 and iOS 17. The dashboard figure is a simulator run from 2026-10-06."
    lede="The fleet operator. One person, more than one rover, native on Mac and iPhone."
    figureLabel="Fig. 08 — Mac dashboard"
    figureAlt="ARGOS on macOS during a geographic simulator run, with a rover pose and an accepted goal."
    poster="/media/argos/macos-dashboard.jpg"
    posterWidth={1200}
    posterHeight={910}
    figureNote="Simulator, 2026-10-06"
    primary={{ href: "#quick-start", label: "Get Started" }}
    secondary={{ href: docs, label: "Read the Docs" }}
    aboutTitle="Supervision, not the vehicle."
    sectionId="quick-start"
    sectionTitle="Quick start."
    sectionLede="Rust tests run anywhere. The app needs a Mac with Xcode. The figure above is a simulator session, not a physical rover."
    links={[
        { href: docs, label: "ARGOS manual" },
        { href: repo, label: "ARGOS on GitHub" },
        { href: "/projects/zorvane", label: "Zorvane" },
        { href: "/projects/terra", label: "Terra" },
    ]}
>
    <Fragment slot="prose">
        <p>
            ARGOS, the Adaptive Robotic Group Operator System, is a native
            fleet operator for <a href="/projects/terra">Terra</a>. Rust and
            Swift, joined by UniFFI. Terra is the vehicle.
            <a href="/projects/zorvane">Zorvane</a> is the world. They meet
            on the <code>terra/rover</code> prefix. Terra owns sensing,
            onboard autonomy, and low-level control.
            ARGOS owns fleet supervision and high-level goals. The Apple
            dashboard is SwiftUI and MapKit over a shared Rust Zenoh client.
            It supplies four autonomy levels, supervised proposal approval,
            per-rover and fleet takeover, emergency stop, held keyboard or
            touch teleop, and session-log export. It discovers rovers, shows
            live positions and goal progress, and sends latched waypoint and
            cancel commands.
        </p>
        <p>
            The dashboard is that native Apple app. Deployment floors are macOS 14 and
            iOS 17, on Apple Silicon: Mac, device, and simulator. The
            screenshot is the Mac dashboard from a geographic simulator run
            on 2026-10-06, against the local Bevy world and the bundled GMU
            tile anchor. It shows a live pose and an accepted goal. It is not
            a physical rover or a physical phone.
        </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="facts">
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Mac</dt>
            <dd>macOS 14, scheme ARGOSMac</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Phone</dt>
            <dd>iOS 17, scheme ARGOSiOS</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Prefix</dt>
            <dd><code>terra/rover</code></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Endpoint</dt>
            <dd><code>tcp/127.0.0.1:7447</code></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Anchor</dt>
            <dd>38.8297, −77.3075</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Evidence</dt>
            <dd>Simulator, 2026-10-06</dd>
        </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="steps">
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">01</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Start the world</h3>
                <p>
                    Clone <a href="/projects/zorvane">Zorvane</a> with Git LFS.
                    The geographic demo uses its bundled GMU anchor:
                </p>
                <pre><code>cd /path/to/Zorvane
TERRA_ROVER_COUNT=1 TERRA_TILES=1 TERRA_TILES_FETCH=0 cargo run -p zorvane</code></pre>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">02</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Connect and send a waypoint</h3>
                <p>
                    In ARGOS, open <span class="act">Connection</span>. Endpoint
                    <code>tcp/127.0.0.1:7447</code>. Prefix
                    <code>terra/rover</code>. Turn the geographic map on.
                    Keep the anchor at latitude 38.8297, longitude −77.3075.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Select the rover. Choose <span class="act">Waypoint</span>.
                    Send 38.82981, −77.3075. You should see the token, then
                    active, then arrived. Cancel, and wait for idle. The bus
                    does not advertise the anchor. If the numbers disagree,
                    the goal lands on the wrong patch.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Flat world: turn geographic mode off and type local metres.
                    +x is north. +y is west.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">03</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Build the app</h3>
                <p>
                    The app needs a Mac with Xcode. Rust edition 2024. Device
                    builds need your signing team. Allow local network on the
                    phone. The Mac target already has outgoing network.
                </p>
                <pre><code>rustup target add aarch64-apple-darwin aarch64-apple-ios aarch64-apple-ios-sim
gem install xcodeproj
./scripts/build-apple.sh
open apps/apple/ARGOS.xcodeproj</code></pre>
                <p>
                    Open scheme <code>ARGOSMac</code> or <code>ARGOSiOS</code>.
                    A physical phone needs the computer’s LAN address, not
                    phone localhost. Start Zorvane with
                    <code>TERRA_ZENOH_LISTEN=tcp/0.0.0.0:7447</code> and enter
                    <code>tcp/COMPUTER_LAN_IP:7447</code>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">04</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Mission smoke, on another port</h3>
                <p>
                    Keep this off the interactive simulator. The example walks
                    autonomy levels, a waypoint, a supervised approval, fleet
                    takeover, and fleet stop.
                </p>
                <pre><code>TERRA_MISSION=1 TERRA_ZENOH_LISTEN=tcp/127.0.0.1:7448 cargo run -p zorvane
cargo run -p argos-zenoh --example mission_smoke -- tcp/127.0.0.1:7448</code></pre>
                <p>
                    Rust checks do not need the simulator:
                    <code>cargo test --workspace --locked</code>. The
                    evidence note from 2026-10-06 records 13 Rust tests and 3
                    unit tests each on macOS and iOS. macOS XCUITest could not
                    initialize host automation, and that UI result is not
                    counted as passing.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
    </Fragment>
</ProjectPage>
